WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.082 --> 00:00:01.620
(pensive music)

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:05.060
- [Mike] This is the 2023 Cadillac LYRIQ.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:06.670
Not only is the LYRIQ General Motors'

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:08.500
first all-electric Cadillac,

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:09.870
the vehicle represents the beginning

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:12.263
of a major strategic
shift for the company.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.810
- Cadillac needs to change.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.850
- [Mike] From a manufacturer
of gas-powered vehicles

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:20.840
to an automaker with
an all-electric fleet,

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.250
built on what GM is saying

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:24.310
is a revolutionary new battery system.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:27.660
- Because when it comes to
this all-electric future.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.530
- Nobody.
- Nobody.

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.920
- Nobody.
- Nobody will be left out.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.573
- And that changes everything.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:34.740
- America's auto industry

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.060
has long known that the
single biggest barrier

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:41.090
to widespread electric vehicle
adoption is battery cost.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:42.720
The theory goes that the companies

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.630
that can bring down their battery prices

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:47.130
will be able to create
more affordable EVs.

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:49.070
And therefore, stand a better chance

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:52.960
of being competitive in the
race for America's EV market.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:54.950
And General Motors' president Mark Reuss

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:56.980
says his company is in a position

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:57.830
to win that race

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.120
with its new battery system.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.010
You guys have been clear

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:03.110
that you're gonna need a
ton of battery capacity.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:05.245
- I think the battery is, for now,

00:01:05.245 --> 00:01:08.230
at the core of cost.

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:09.890
To vertically integrate that

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:11.585
and to actually develop our own chemistry

00:01:11.585 --> 00:01:13.393
is a competitive advantage.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:15.640
- And that's what's brought me here

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.963
to GM's test track in Milford, Michigan.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.140
When it first opened in 1924,

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:22.180
the Milford proving grounds

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:25.043
was the industry's first
automobile testing facility.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.490
Today I've come to this 4,000-acre track

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:30.070
to ride along in a test version

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:31.500
of a vehicle that GM says

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.340
will play a pivotal role in its future.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:34.760
Let's check this thing out.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.300
This LYRIQ isn't quite
ready for the showroom.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.760
What you're seeing is a model
still under development,

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.140
but with the vehicle's launch
date approaching next spring,

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:43.100
Reuss offered to give me

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.350
an exclusive behind-the-scenes look.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:48.950
So let's talk about how
important the LYRIQ is

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:51.210
just for GM broadly.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.650
This is the first fully electric Cadillac,

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:55.330
almost 120-year history, right?

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:56.190
That's a big deal.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:57.200
- First is first.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.200
This is the first no-kidding
mainstream transformation

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.660
of Cadillac and the company.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.450
It's all happening with this car.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:08.090
- [Narrator] It's a
reflection of what's possible.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:11.090
- [Mike] In January,
General Motors set 2035

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:13.110
as the target date for when it hopes

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:16.231
to complete its transition to EVs.

00:02:16.231 --> 00:02:18.170
Currently, 98% of GM sales

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.570
are vehicles that run on fossil fuels,

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:21.910
and the company's bestselling cars

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:24.630
are large pickup trucks
and sport utility vehicles

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.170
that are among its least fuel-efficient.

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.300
GM's brands, Chevrolet, Buick,

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.750
GMC and Cadillac have loyal followings

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:33.300
but it isn't clear if those buyers

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.480
or new ones will follow GM

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:37.229
into its EV future.

00:02:37.229 --> 00:02:38.230
(upbeat music)

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.260
GM has also lagged behind in a market

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.490
where two competitors, Tesla and Ford,

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:45.730
are already doing well
with new upscale SUVs.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:47.690
- [Narrator] It's more than
cutting-edge technology.

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:49.310
It's the spirit of it.

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:50.630
It's a Mustang.

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:52.020
- Do you think this is a Litmus test?

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.080
If this car turns out as good as you think

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:55.430
and expect that it will,

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:57.640
should we see that kind of acceptance

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.780
for a car that doesn't
have a Tesla badge on it?

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:00.613
- I think so.

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:03.570
We made the investment
across the whole portfolio.

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:08.360
All the engineering, all of the
component sets are Cadillac,

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:09.830
and I think people are gonna see that.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:13.050
And they're gonna see it as the
tip of the technology spear.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:14.650
- [Mike] GM has a long history

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:16.410
of innovation in the EV space,

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:17.863
dating back to 1912

00:03:17.863 --> 00:03:20.200
when the company built electric trucks

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.850
with lead acid, and nickel-iron batteries.

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:25.300
In the mid-1990s, GM developed the EV1,

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:28.210
which was the first
mass-produced electric vehicle

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:30.580
in the modern era from a major automaker.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:32.543
The car could only be
leased in a few cities,

00:03:32.543 --> 00:03:35.647
and GM abandoned the
model after a few years.

00:03:35.647 --> 00:03:38.060
(car whirring)

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:39.660
The modern race for EV dominance

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:42.160
didn't really begin
until about 15 years ago

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.920
when Tesla arrived on the
scene with its Roadster.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:46.060
- I'm really excited by the fact

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.800
that we've got a zero-emissions sports car

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.310
that can go head to head

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.240
with a Ferrari and a Porsche, and win.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:55.940
- [Mike] In 2010, GM helped
pioneer plug-in hybrid EVs

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:57.980
with its compact Chevy Volt.

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:01.300
- General Motors is getting
back into the battery business.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:02.330
- [Mike] The Volt was marketed

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:04.770
as a practical and affordable hybrid

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:07.600
but was never a big commercial success.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:10.870
GM didn't enter today's
EV market until 2016

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:12.710
when it released the Chevy Bolt.

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:16.240
- This is truly an EV for everyone.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:17.270
For most people,

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:19.890
this can be their everyday driver.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.790
- [Mike] The Bolt offered
more than 200 miles of range

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:23.270
for the relatively affordable price

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:27.210
of $30,000 but sales
have lagged behind Tesla,

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:30.560
and recently the Bolt has
become a costly headache for GM.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:33.790
This August, the company
expanded a previous safety recall

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:35.070
for a fire risk related

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:38.590
to lithium ion batteries
supplied by Korea's LG.

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:41.530
The recall covers all
of the 142,000 Volts GM

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:45.143
has built at an estimated
cost of $1.8 billion.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:48.090
- Those are very serious
things that happened.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:50.910
If you're putting the
customer at the center,

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:52.960
like we do, of safety,

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:54.910
and if we have a problem, in this case

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:57.480
in a supplier who is
making the sale for us,

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.170
we bring the bare General
Motors technical expertise

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:01.570
to go in and solve that,

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:03.420
and make it right for the customer.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:05.380
- Today, the US EV market

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:08.780
still makes up only about
3% of all car sales.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:11.340
And Tesla, which sold
about two of every three

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:13.930
all-electric vehicles purchased in 2020,

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:16.640
remains the sector's dominant player.

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:18.540
But other automakers' commitment to EVs

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.600
has intensified in the past year.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:21.790
More than a dozen new models

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:24.030
are set to arrive in
US showrooms this year

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:25.266
as startups like Rivian,

00:05:25.266 --> 00:05:28.001
and established automakers
like Ford and Volkswagen

00:05:28.001 --> 00:05:31.720
show the results of billions
in technology spending.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:34.030
Just in the last six to nine months,

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:35.640
we've heard a lot of announcements

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:38.320
from some of your rivals getting
into the battery business.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:39.700
And everyone's kind of reading

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:40.840
from the same playbook now.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.770
So what's gonna separate
the winners from the losers?

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:47.140
- I think until you get
dedicated platforms for EV

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:50.650
and not derivative scarred architectures

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:53.810
that carry the inefficiencies

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:55.240
of an internal combustion engine

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:59.030
into an EV platform is one
of the key differentiators.

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:00.730
- [Mike] Until recently, many EVs

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:03.130
were built on car platforms
originally developed

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:05.700
for gas-powered or hybrid vehicles.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:07.090
But because the mechanical layout

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:08.900
of electric cars is so different,

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:11.100
that has meant that many
EVs have been constrained

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:12.720
in their design.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:16.010
GM, which is America's
largest automaker by sales,

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:19.020
is betting big on its new
Ultium battery system.

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:21.840
- [Narrator] Fewer wires
within the pack allows room

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:23.250
for more batteries,

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:25.540
helping extend charging ranges.

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:27.410
- [Mike] The technology
uses a different chemistry

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:29.750
and design than the
early GM battery systems

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:32.460
on the Chevy Bolt and Chevy Volt.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.900
Automakers are now transitioning to EVs

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:36.940
with dedicated EV platforms,

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:38.630
like GM's Ultium system.

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:40.680
While expensive, these platforms offer

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:41.890
more design flexibility

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:45.500
without some of the
inefficiencies of past EVs.

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:48.080
- With cars like the LYRIQ here,

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:49.970
we're gonna have over 300 miles of range.

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:53.780
We're gonna have the ability
to charge very quickly.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:54.990
That's the kind of flexibility

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.770
that gives a competitive advantage

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:59.580
if you have the best chemistry

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:01.910
and you have it vertically
integrated on scale.

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:03.340
And that's what we're doing.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:06.660
- [Mike] Today, many electric
car owners use their EVs

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:08.220
as a secondary vehicle

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:11.250
because charging times
limit use on longer trips.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:12.980
But Reuss is confident the LYRIQ,

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:14.820
and other future GM electrics

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:16.550
will mark the beginning of a sea change

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:18.640
in how drivers approach EVs.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:22.331
- If you're offering things
that are $10,000 more

00:07:22.331 --> 00:07:24.740
on a vehicle that isn't as functional

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:26.510
and can't be a primary vehicle,

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:27.343
and you don't know

00:07:27.343 --> 00:07:29.080
how to get that kind of charge time on it,

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:30.450
that's kind of a no-go.

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:32.620
The whole mission was to get people

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:35.210
to consider it as a primary vehicle,

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:37.137
and then you see things
really start to change.

00:07:37.137 --> 00:07:39.804
(pensive music)

